<ContentPage
  title="Cookie Statement | LazyTools"
  description="LazyTools uses Google Analytics cookies to measure page visits and shows third-party ads (Adsterra) that may set their own advertising cookies. The files and values you enter into a tool never reach a server. How to limit or opt out."
  heading="Cookie Statement"
  updated="October 25, 2026"
>
  <p>
    LazyTools uses cookies in two places: <strong>our own analytics</strong> (Google Analytics), set only if you
    accept the cookie banner, and <strong>a third-party advertising network (Adsterra)</strong> whose ads keep the
    tools free and which may set its own cookies. Crucially, neither touches the content of the tools:
    <strong>the files and values you enter into any tool are processed in your browser and never reach a server</strong>,
    so they are never available to us, to Google, or to any advertiser. The tools work the same whatever you choose.
  </p>

  <h2>1. Our analytics cookies (first-party)</h2>
  <p>
    Once you accept the banner, Google Analytics (GA4) sets these to measure page visits and engagement. They are
    off by default (Google Consent Mode, denied until you consent), so if you decline or ignore the banner, none
    are set. They record how the site is used, never what you enter into a tool.
  </p>
  <div class="overflow-x-auto">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Cookie</th><th>Purpose</th><th>Duration</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>_ga</code></td><td>Distinguishes individual visitors (a pseudonymous ID)</td><td>2 years</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>_ga_&lt;id&gt;</code></td><td>Persists session state for our GA4 property</td><td>2 years</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>2. Advertising cookies (third-party, Adsterra)</h2>
  <p>
    To keep every tool free, LazyTools shows ads served by <strong>Adsterra</strong>, a third-party advertising
    network. When an ad loads, Adsterra and its advertising partners may set their own cookies and use similar
    technologies to measure ad performance and target ads, which can include <strong>cross-site targeting</strong>.
    These cookies are set by Adsterra, not by us, and we do not control or have access to them. Some ad formats on
    the site are more intrusive than a plain banner: a <strong>pop-under</strong> may open an advertiser's page in a
    separate tab, a <strong>floating social bar</strong> may appear, and Adsterra may <strong>ask your permission to
    send browser push notifications</strong> (you can always decline, and revoke it in your browser's site
    settings). None of these can see the files or values you enter into a tool.
  </p>
  <p>
    Adsterra describes its own data practices in its <a href="https://adsterra.com/privacy-policy/" rel="noopener nofollow">privacy policy</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>What isn't a cookie</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Local storage, kept locally.</strong> Some tools remember your preferences or saved data, Pomodoro settings, Kanban boards, a saved unit, whether you've rated a tool, using your browser's own local storage. This is <em>not</em> a cookie: it stays on your device, is never transmitted to us, and you can clear it any time via your browser settings.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What we still don't do</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>We never upload, store or sell the files and values you enter into a tool, they do not leave your device.</li>
    <li>We do not share your tool content with advertisers or anyone else, because we never receive it in the first place.</li>
    <li>We do not set our own advertising cookies; ad cookies come from Adsterra under its own policy.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How to opt out or limit cookies</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Analytics:</strong> decline the cookie banner, install Google's <a href="https://tools.google.com/dlpage/gaoptout" rel="noopener">Analytics Opt-out Add-on</a>, or block analytics cookies in your browser.</li>
    <li><strong>Advertising:</strong> use your browser's tracking protection or a content/ad blocker, block third-party cookies, and decline the push-notification prompt if it appears. Clearing cookies removes identifiers already stored.</li>
    <li><strong>Everywhere:</strong> private/incognito windows and most privacy-focused browsers limit or discard these cookies automatically.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Full details are in the <a href="/privacy/#optout">privacy policy</a>.</p>

  <h2>A note on server logs</h2>
  <p>
    Loading any website means your browser makes a request to a server, and, like every host, ours keeps
    standard access logs (see the <a href="/privacy/">privacy policy</a>). That's ordinary web infrastructure,
    not a cookie.
  </p>

  <h2>Questions</h2>
  <p>
    Every tool on this site works exactly the same with or without cookies or analytics, because the tools don't
    depend on them. Questions? <a href="/contact/">Contact us</a>.
  </p>
</ContentPage>
